Preparation for compilation via crystal lang:

We will need "native", i.e. crystal, implementations of core ruby classes, notably String and Hash.

String: ruby string semantics are different to crystal - mutability, encoding in particular with special-casing of 8-bit encodings, and ruby's ability to cope with invalid strings of a purported encoding. The correct way to implement this is probably to write a from-scratch RubyString class in crystal that follows MRI impl fairly directly. MRI ruby generates encoding translators from declarative mapping files - there is a tool to do this. We can steal the encoding definition classes from MRI ruby and "port" the translator generators to generate crystal code.

Hash: ruby has the unusual semantics that iterators follow insertion order. This will probably have to be pasted onto existing crystal hash-map behaviour.

Integer: ruby integer is interesting because it auto-promotes to big-num. Not sure what crystal supports in this respect. Crystal does have OverflowError, which should make auto-promotion quite easy. And a BigInt ligrary.